Software the installer fetches, rather than ships
  The installer downloads these at install time, checks them against a pinned checksum or a lockfile hash, and runs them under
  their own licenses; our downloads do not contain them:
    - Node.js (pinned version, from nodejs.org): MIT, with its bundled third-party components under their own licenses.
    - The engine's and the sandbox provider's npm dependencies, from their lockfiles (see THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md), including:
        . the embedded PostgreSQL binaries (PostgreSQL License);
        . the sharp image library's libvips builds (LGPL-3.0-or-later): used as a separate, replaceable library;
        . @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk and @cursor/sdk, which declare "SEE LICENSE IN ..." and are under their own terms,
          not an open-source license. They are the engine's dependencies, not ours. Anyone who redistributes an image that
          bundles them must read and follow those terms first.

If you build an image that bundles the engine
  Redistributing the engine's installed packages (an installer image, a container image) makes you the distributor of every
  package above. Check each license in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md, keep every notice, offer the source of copyleft components, and
  read the terms of the packages that say "SEE LICENSE IN ...". docs/PINOS.md describes the plan for such an image.
